# Anti-Pattern Rule

The agent must avoid common Unity and software architecture anti-patterns.

Avoid:

- God Class
- Massive MonoBehaviour
- Manager Hell
- Singleton Abuse
- Static State Abuse
- Tight Coupling
- Circular Dependency
- Spaghetti Logic
- Copy-Paste Code
- Magic Numbers
- Hard-coded References
- Deep Nested If Statements
- Large Switch Statements
- Hidden Side Effects
- Large Update Loops
- Business Logic inside UI classes
- Business Logic inside MonoBehaviour when it can be separated
- Public Fields Everywhere
- Scene-dependent logic that cannot be reused
- Direct dependency between unrelated systems

If the existing codebase contains these problems, the agent must:

- Report the issue before changing code.
- Explain the impact.
- Suggest a safe refactor plan.
- Avoid making the problem worse.